WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.210
<v ->And so next, let's talk about AppStream 2.0.</v>

2
00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.450
This is a desktop application streaming service.

3
00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.730
The idea is that you're gonna have a desktop application,

4
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.550
but you want to deliver it to any computer

5
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.340
without acquiring or provisioning infrastructure.

6
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.310
The way this works is that this app is going to be delivered

7
00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.260
from within a web browser,

8
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.300
and this is why it's called AppStream,

9
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.940
because your web browser is going to stream the application

10
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.610
that's going to be run, but from your web browser.

11
00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.170
I'll give you a very graphical example,

12
00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:31.740
so I used the AppStream service to open up Blender,

13
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.630
which is a desktop application,

14
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.450
and as you can see, Blender was streaming

15
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:40.830
from somewhere into my Chrome web browser,

16
00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.990
and I was able to use Blender as is.

17
00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.870
So AppStream is really to stream applications,

18
00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:49.260
whereas Workspaces is to get a true desktop

19
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.660
on which you can run any application you want.

20
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.440
So let's compare AppStream's 2.0 and Workspaces.

21
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.160
Workspaces is a fully managed VDI and desktop.

22
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.720
The users will connect to the VDI

23
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.000
and open native applications,

24
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.770
or applications that have been packaged

25
00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:08.190
using Workspace Application Manager, or WAM.

26
00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.080
The Workspaces can be on demand

27
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.050
and then it will turn off whenever we're done, or always on,

28
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.290
whereas AppStream's 2.0 is going to be to stream

29
00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.230
a desktop application into your web browsers,

30
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.360
and so you don't need to connect to a VDI,

31
00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.780
and it works with really any device that has a web browser,

32
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.930
so you can stream a desktop application for Windows

33
00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:32.070
onto a Mac device through a Chrome web browser, for example.

34
00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.870
And the cool thing is that each

35
00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:37.020
of these AppStream applications can be configured

36
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.080
to run on an instance type per application type.

37
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.210
So maybe if you're running something like Photoshop,

38
00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.520
you need more CPU, more RAM, and more GPU,

39
00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.160
you can configure your AppStream container

40
00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.630
to have more of that,

41
00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.190
and so that gives you full control

42
00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:53.250
over how much power should be allocated

43
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.500
to the application that you're streaming.

44
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.870
And if you make the differences between the two,

45
00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.210
between Workspaces and AppStream 2.0,

46
00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.890
then you're good to go for the exam.

47
00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:02.880
All right, that's it.

48
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.630
I will see you in the next lecture.

